* pk_durability_regression.dts -- AIF-156.
*
* THE ONLY SPEC IN THIS TREE THAT MEASURES SOMETHING ACROSS A PROCESS BOUNDARY,
* and it is a THIN WRAPPER on purpose. It asserts almost nothing itself.
*
* A .dts RUNS IN ONE PROCESS. That is not a gap in PKPOLICY, it is a limit of
* the instrument, and PKPOLICY's own header says so under NOT CLAIMED: no
* marker in it can tell whether the PRIMARY designation was READ BACK from the
* x64 header or merely remembered in a map that had not died yet. Until
* 2026-09-07 the answer was the second, and a fresh session let REPLACE
* overwrite a primary key IN SILENCE on a build where all three PKPOLICY arms
* read green.
*
* SO THE WORK HAPPENS IN TWO CHILD PROCESSES AND THE GRADING HAPPENS IN C++.
* `!` runs std::system(), so a child's stdout never passes through the stream
* the routed capture swaps -- a transcript-reading validator would see NONE of
* the child markers. The children write theirs through SET ALTERNATE and
* validate_pk_durability() READS THOSE FILES OFF DISK. A validator can open a
* file; a marker cannot. That asymmetry is the whole design.
*
* WHAT THIS FILE CONTRIBUTES is the one thing the validator cannot get from the
* captures: evidence that the shell-out was REACHED AT ALL. If the `!` line
* fails -- no PowerShell, no built exe, an execution policy -- the captures are
* simply absent, and absent is indistinguishable from "the children ran and
* wrote nothing". PKDUR_G0 prints before the launch and PKDUR_G1 after it, so a
* transcript with G0 and no G1 says the shell-out died, which is a different
* finding from a durability failure and must not be reported as one.
*
* NOTHING HERE ERASES ANYTHING. pk_durable_write.dts leaves its table behind on
* purpose -- it must outlive its process or there is nothing to reopen -- and
* pk_durable_read.dts erases it. This file owns neither.

SET TALK OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

FORMULA "PKDURABLE-BEGIN"

* PKDUR_G0 -- reached the launch. A literal .T. rather than a field read,
* because there is no table open here and this spec deliberately opens none;
* the claim is only "control arrived at this line".
? "PKDUR_G0_reached_the_shellout:" + (1 = 1)

* The launcher self-locates from its own directory, so this path is relative to
* the runtime data root the shell was started in and nothing here hardcodes a
* drive letter. It copies no binary and sets no environment -- see its header
* for why going through datarun.ps1 would try to overwrite the running exe.
! powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ..\..\tools\staging\pk_durability_child.ps1

* PKDUR_G1 -- the shell-out returned. Says nothing about what it found.
? "PKDUR_G1_shellout_returned:" + (1 = 1)

FORMULA "PKDURABLE-END"
